  "report_markdown": "DRAFT\n\n**Complaint/History**\n- Patient reports a 3- to 4-day history of itchy, sore, red skin primarily affecting the chest, inner elbows, and hands.\n- Symptoms are severe enough to disrupt sleep and concentration at work.\n- Describes skin as dry, cracked in some areas, but no bleeding or discharge.\n- Symptoms differ from prior eczema episodes, which were limited to the arms.\n- Over-the-counter steroid cream (referred to as \"sterile cream\") and antihistamines tried without relief.\n- No recent changes in shower gel, clothing, or known exposure to sick contacts; no fever, respiratory, gastrointestinal, or urinary symptoms.\n\n**Relevant Background**\n- Past history of eczema, previously managed with unspecified cream.\n- Past history of asthma, not currently active.\n- No regular medications.\n- No known allergies.\n- Works as a project manager in a pharmaceutical company.\n- Lives with flatmates; non-smoker; consumes alcohol occasionally (a few beers per week).\n\n**Findings**\n- Rash described as red, itchy, sore, with dry and cracked skin.\n- Distribution: chest, inner elbows, hands. Not present on face.\n- No bleeding, pus, or systemic symptoms.\n- Clinician notes rash appearance is consistent with eczema flare based on description, though sound quality limited full assessment.\n\n**Clinician's Assessment**\n- Likely flare-up of eczema, possibly exacerbated by environmental or irritant triggers.\n- Differential not fully explored due to remote consultation and poor audio quality.\n\n**Plan/Follow-up**\n- Prescribe stronger topical steroid cream.\n- Prescribe emollient (\"ammolians\") for use in bath/shower to moisturize skin; use regularly for 7–10 days.\n- Offer prescription for fexofenadine (stronger antihistamine) to try for itch control.\n- Advised to keep trigger diary (e.g., soaps, fabrics, stress).\n- Follow up in one week if symptoms do not improve.\n- No immediate referral or additional investigations proposed.",
